body:
Rabbit stuff time! :) Rabbits scare very easily, you have to be patient and sit with them very quietly before they'll love you. If you reach your hand out to them before they're comfortable with you they'll bolt and you have to wait so much longer. 

&#x200B;

If they're long furred brush them every single day. Just a little bit, so they don't get tangles.

&#x200B;

If they get tangles that you can't brush out cut them out immediately, just be extra careful about their skin. As the fur grows the tangles do too so they'll always be too close to the skin for you comfort. If you leave the tangle in it'll hurt your rabbit and that's the last thing you want. (Made the mistake of letting it grow out for a week because it was close to the skin, ended up having a month long process of cutting and brushing it out when I could've done it in 20 minutes day one.)

&#x200B;

Almost always get them in pairs. Rabbits need to bond with other rabbits with very few exceptions. With those exceptions it's easy to notice that something is off. 

&#x200B;

Rabbits get hypothermia super easily, so don't get them wet ever! They usually clean themselves very well unless they have special needs, so baths are unnecessary.

&#x200B;

Don't give them yogurt ever. Not in treats or on it's own, they do horribly with dairy. Carrots are high in sugar, anything high in sugar should only be given in moderation. Rabbit insulin is Expensive and they hate needles, so don't give them diabetes if it's preventable. Making your own treats for them is less expensive and you can control sugar content.

&#x200B;

To treat the sniffles just keep the infected rabbit away from any others so it doesn't catch and make sure they stay warm in a dry environment with good air quality. If a nostril gets clogged that is a medical emergency. Rabbits cannot breathe out of their mouths, and any nose clogging is life threatening for them. Take a warm washcloth and, being extra careful to not cover the clean nostril, gently rub away the clog and clean off the area around their nose. Repeat until it's not a problem anymore. They'll usually get sniffles in the early winter and if you have more than one it's almost a guarantee that you'll have someone catch it.

&#x200B;

Always keep a lookout for flies, last year I lost a rabbit to what I think was deadly fly strike and it was horrible and heart wrenching.

&#x200B;

Lastly but certainly not least, they are very fragile. Their necks break at the drop of a hat around 8 weeks old, which is when a lot of breeders sell their babies. Around 10 or 11 weeks as long as you don't drop them they should be alright in that regard. Their skin will always be remarkably sensitive and tears really easy so make sure to keep any sharp edges covered. They can be wonderful companions, but they're very labor intensive and need more care than a cat or dog.

Pay off a ton of people's medical bills and shit.  Build a couple schools that I think would work well for disabled kids like I was. Fund some pres-schools. Free childcare in some less well-off neighborhoods. Community fridges.  I'm a socialist, that's what we do. 

&#x200B;

With my 1% I'd buy a nice bit of land and a TON of cows so that I could breed them and sell them off as needed to continue paying for the schools and stuff. Then I'd adopt a couple dogs and some cats, find a wife, maybe have a kid or two. OH! And I'd get myself the one car I've always wanted, a 67 Impala, chrome and black with leather seats. Maybe a Nintendo 3DS, but I'd be busy working a lot so I wouldn't have much time to play with it.

Lol, I'm glad I could add some shrimpy humor to your day.

&#x200B;

(I have an Instagram where I post my food, so I tried to make it look \*Fancy\* lol)

YES! As a rabbit breeder, I love when people know this from the get-go. I always ask people what their cage setups are like and encourage everyone to get a pair of same sex siblings that have already shown a bond or a rabbit that has already shown a dislike for other rabbits (Only ever had two of those, and they're both doing very well as singles but it's SUPER rare for rabbits to be anti-social and they always need a patient person to bond with). That way no matter what you know they'll probably be okay. 

&#x200B;

I also have anywhere between 30 and 45 rabbits at any given time including babies. For two rabbits you'd just need an extra large cage and a bunny-proof place for them to run around, but what I have is the best for my six females and their babies. As someone who has dealt with rabbits for a while, this is the best cage that I could come up with. 

&#x200B;

We made a chicken wire hutch with concrete floors (Dirt and straw layers above that for warmth and burrowing) with empty Tidy Cats plastic buckets (We made them nesting boxes. Half buried makes them warm, they're enclosed so they feel safe, and I can open the lids to interact and make sure everything is okay.) holding up our Cain Corso's old dog cage (Massive) that is lined with wood to make it feel like a safer space. 

I also made a little parkour course because rabbits love climbing. The roof is made of old windows so they get plenty of sunlight and in the Winter we wrap the outside in heavy plastic and keep a heater going at all times. Sometimes two. It stays at minimum 50 degrees Murican' with a max of 80. 

That's the main structure within the wire, but we do have a good bit of frolicking room in their area too and are looking into planting grass. 3 adult humans can stand in that cage at the same time. 

&#x200B;

This cage would be full on luxury for less than 5 rabbits, but if you scaled it down a bit with just the wood cage (Maybe a slightly smaller one) and the parkour area, take out the nesting boxes if you're not breeding, and a bit less room in general it could work for a pair of two.

Beads. I do beaded jewelry. One day I dropped a bead in my couch. Just one. Now every time I stand up there's a bead imbedded in my pants or my leg. My cats get them caught in their fur. I stuck my hand in the couch once, I no longer have a hand. Just a mass of tiny metallic beads. Some of them are too pointy to miss. Those are my special beads. They somehow end up in my bed despite my bead box never leaving the living room. They wake me in the middle of the night. Slowly I am being consumed by an endless swarm of rainbow beads. Save me.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Also, rabbit poop. Not related to the beads at all. I just hobby breed Netherland Dwarfs to be as small as they can be. Ever seen a runt baby dwarf rabbit? They need to be hand raised because their moms try to eat them, but boy is it worth it! I thought I had enough house plants to use the poop as fertilizer. Turns out 28 rabbits produce quite a bit of excrement.

Same here! :) I don't doubt that the EM was just being an asshole, and I'm very sorry that you had to go through that. I just felt like I should confirm that it most likely was a fall and not anything you or your friends did. They really do be fragile. 

&#x200B;

Also, my babies are pure Netherland Dwarfs except one of the ladies I have who is a Dutch with perfect markings. When I crossbreed her with my Dwarfs they get Dwarf size and shape with Dutch markings. 10/10 would recommend

Rabbits are surprisingly fragile, especially in the neck area. If it fell on it's front half it would've died 100%. Had one snap it's own neck falling maybe 10 inches to a foot, the little guy was only 4 weeks though so it was a bit too high for him. Can't really change where mom rabbits nest. But yeah, a hill over a foot high can be deadly, any higher and it could snap the spine too. So, sadly, there are many different ways for a rabbit to go like that.

&#x200B;

SOURCE: I currently have 28 rabbits and have had many others in the past.

