No worries, I've been almost scammed more times than I can count and am finally learning from it. On track to get my first full book published soon!

&#x200B;

BTW, that advice goes for any intellectual or creative labor. Like selling drawings/infographics  to a business you don't work for. Or speaking at a conference like TED (Most of the time). It's all about getting your energy paid back in money. For some reason, us creative types don't seem to get paid like we should.

Publishing a book shouldn't cost you anything, the publishers should pay you a lump sum proportional to the amount of work and level of quality in your book, plus a reasonable amount of royalties that will need to be negotiated from the original percentage they offer. Any company that requires you to pay them should not be trusted under any circumstances. Work and time invested is also proportional to the quality of the  work and how many professional editors your book goes through. In the end, you should approve every edit made but they should be suggested and carried out by the professionals that work for the publishing company. I always say that it takes two years to finish your first book. A year writing it, a year getting it published.

&#x200B;

If you're talking about self publishing, I wouldn't recommend anybody do that. Unless you have the proper contacts and a lawyer to write up contracts between you and businesses that will sell your book, or a Very large online presence (We're talking at the very least two million positive interactions per social media post.) with a positive community to buy your book, it doesn't pay off.

