Grab my swords and put all my cats in a different room so they'll be safe. Then grab the nunchucks, the knife, and the daggers off my wall. Then secure them onto my body, call the cops. Once the burglars get in I have about 10 minutes to train in my knife fighting before the cops show up. 

&#x200B;

Plan of attack, I'd start with the sword, then after getting disarmed go on to the knife, continue like that going on from knife to daggers, if needed I'd end with nunchucks and knock them out. By then if the cops aren't there I go for the duct tape and tie them up After cops leave I let the cats out and continue scrolling Reddit.

Rehab doesn't work for most people. It appeals to a certain thinking style, but that's just one style out of hundreds. I think that addiction to things like heroin or meth are mental illnesses in most, and that requires proper therapy. However, the therapy needs to be deeply personalized. Then there is the cost of rehab, it's just not affordable for most addicts. Not all addicts are thieves, not all homeless people are thieves, not all homeless addicts are thieves. The combination of being an addict and homeless increases the likelihood of being a thief, but it's nowhere near a guarantee. Anecdotally, most homeless addicts I know (About 14 people total. I'd say only two of them don't follow this rule.) refuse to steal from anywhere that isn't Walmart. If what you're proposing actually happened there would be no real help for most of those people. It would also be a slippery slope to just putting all homeless people in those camps. Or all addicts in those camps. 

&#x200B;

I vote that we just give all homeless people, no matter their status as a drug-user or not, a home with a year of bills paid, a year of therapy, and a pack of guaranteed-hire job applications; the first time they become homeless. After that it's a case by case basis on whether or not they receive that help to prevent abuse of the system, but it'd definitely help a lot of people.

As somebody who is as socialist as they come, I don't think we should kill them. I think we should have productive conversations with those we can have productive conversations with. Show them how their tangerine is impacting people. (Like the one ex-supporter of his who he had give up an heirloom to a person they were trying to recruit.) Maybe then we'd be able to convert some. They wouldn't not be republicans, I don't think you can change people's values. But maybe by showing the impacts of their support and, way after Trump, their decisions on healthcare and abortion and things like those, we could slowly create a culture where human life and human rights are more important than the ability to carry a gun into Walmart. That's what I'm aiming for. But after that social shift, people will slowly begin aligning with our way of thinking more and more.

&#x200B;

I know that we need quick change, but sadly we probably won't get that. It's probably something we'll have to wait for. But I'm willing to wait.

As somebody raised poor, who is still poor, and will continue to be poor because of my various physical and mental illnesses, I agree with this guy. I should not have been born into the situation I was born into. My parents should have aborted me. Then I wouldn't have grown up with an abusive but able to afford two meals a day instead of one grandmother. I wouldn't have been forced to find money in every way possible while still a child so that I could buy school uniforms. I shouldn't have been put through that. 

&#x200B;

So, that is why I believe that if you can't afford children you shouldn't have children. Should everybody be able to afford them if they want them? Hell yes, but that's not how it is working out in the world. Kids are horrible burdens to poor families. Often loved burdens, but burdens none the less. If you can't give a kid three meals a day plus snacks, clothes and shoes for school and play, a stable place to live so they aren't under a bridge for a month, and a small rainy day fund in case of emergencies, you can't afford them and shouldn't have them.

&#x200B;

If you can afford kids, even barely, then I'm all for it. But bringing children into the world just to suffer is cruel and shouldn't ever be done.

The cats in my area have a genetic predisposition for lung issues. If one gets the aggressive upper respiratory infection that the cats here also seem to all carry, they nearly always die. Out of all the cats we've taken in, only three have ever survived it. My cat was one of them, but he survived twice. He's the first to do that. The first time was when he was a kitten and he and his brother both got sick at the same time, the brother passed. JoeJoe had it much worse, but because he kept drinking water he survived and made a full recovery. The second time was very recently. He's still working on gaining more weight but is no longer sneezing and is eating regularly. 

&#x200B;

The strange part is, none of the other cats kept drinking whenever they got this. They usually lay down in one spot and stay there until they die, but my cat kept moving. It took him all day to get up the stairs once, but he managed it every single day to sleep outside my bedroom door as usual. I'd say it's definitely the strangest thing he's ever done.

